y  jobs also have simply been eliminated by  automation and by robotics without  actually going to another country and as  far as the bringing back of the jobs  that at some point did leave to other  countries a lot of those jobs have since  been automated away by automation or  robotics so when you look at a decision  that's made by Trump like this first of  all it's going to impact products made  with steel and aluminum right consumers  don't buy much steel or aluminum  directly but many of the products we buy  and businesses we engage with do buy  those materials now number two other  countries can also do things as Donald  Trump likes to say in retaliation to  these tariffs other countries including  the European Union which is a group of  countries of course can take steps that  would be terrible for us as a result of  this and in fact the President of the  European Commission jean-claude Juncker  has already said quote if the Americans  impose tariffs on steel and aluminium  then we must treat American products the  same way we will put tariffs on  harley-davidson on Bourbon and on blue  jeans Levi's the Canadian Prime Minister  Justin Trudeau said that the u.s.  putting in place these tariffs would be  absolutely unacceptable China expressed  grave concern that's a quote over the  plans and you also have to consider sort  of how fundamentally backwards the  overarching themes of the administration  are right there's a total refusal to  impose the sanctions that the House and  Senate approved for Russia as a  punishment for things they actually did  and meanwhile Trump is gleeful on  Twitter about implementing what are sort  of de facto sanctions right economic  sanctions in a sense on the European  Union and on Canada which by the way the  largest exporter of steel to the United  States is Canada now there are sort of  two debates here two debates that can be  had one debate generally is free trade  versus protectionism and you've got the  free trade side which says  trade opens markets it reduces prices  for consumers which has some truth to it  and of course there are downsides to  free trade free trade degrades the  rights of workers in many ways it can  have a downward influence on wages it's  arguably terrible for the environment  and in more than one way actually the  protectionism side likes to argue that  tariffs and trade restrictions  restrictions by countries protect those  countries jobs that country's jobs but  that they do raise prices so you keep  jobs but stuff costs more I believe that  as it relates to Trump's tariffs it's  less about free trade versus  protectionism than it is about trade  Wars because as it stands what Trump  will do if indeed he carries carries  these tariffs out start a trade war  right and there's no evidence at all  that trade wars are good for people the  impact of a trade war inevitably is that  you increase prices for US consumers on  a whole bunch of products as well as for  businesses it will lead to a loss of  sales for American businesses because  they are going to be exporting less to  other countries as a result of that  retaliatory effect and then you will see  that those lost contracts for American  companies take a long time to get back  if you do two years of this stuff and  then you cancel it for example you don't  get all those contracts back for  American businesses it actually takes a  long time so what do we ultimately  circle back to well we circle back to  that in 2018  lots of political and economic issues  require collective solutions I've talked  about this before Donald Trump's entire  America first thing aside from being  sort of a reminiscence calling-card of  really horrible movements that use that  same phrase Trump's America first thing  ignores sort of the political realities  of 2018 right the issue of climate  change the idea that an individual  country can solve climate change is  ridiculous you can't have a nationalist  solution to climate change you can have  individual countries working together  or a world solution but pollution  doesn't respect country borders the jet  stream and the way that the tides in the  ocean work aren't going to say oh we're  crossing over into another country's  oceans well no we're not going to do  that so you don't have to worry about  the plastic in our oceans for example  you need collective solutions Trump ran  on acting unilaterally in the interest  of the United States it turns out it's  just incredibly naive and childish in  today's globalized economy like it or  not right whatever your position is it  is naive and childish to pretend or to  argue or assert that an individual  country can sort of unilaterally solve  some of these major problems and this  gets me back once again sucks that we  have to go back to it quite frankly to  the sabotaging of relationships by  Donald Trump and of the reputation of  the United States around the world is  there any possible upside to this the  only upside is that Trump has said he's  going to do this but it's probably  possible that he could be talked out of  it and there's only one possible  indication of that this morning Donald  Trump tweeted we have large trade  deficits with Mexico and Canada NAFTA  which is under renegotiation right now  has been a bad deal for the u.s. a  massive relocation of companies and jobs  tariffs on steel and aluminum will only  come off if new and fair NAFTA agreement  is signed and then he goes on to talk  about Canada so there's a couple ways we  could interpret that one is that Trump  is just generally clueless he tweeted  after Wilbur Ross proposed these tariffs  Trump said he's gonna put the tariffs in  now somebody went back to him and said  hey you know what this is actually gonna  do a lot of damage you may want to just  consider the possibility of not doing it  and Trump has now come up with a new way  he's sort of qualifying the tariffs by  saying if we get a NAFTA renegotiation  that he likes would even understand that  I guess if someone tells him it's a good  renegotiation he would back off of it  but this is not a good direction to go  in and we've seen it not that the stock  market  is the be-all end-all of how the economy  is doing but the fact that the stock  market has been both crashing and  erratically swinging like a yo-yo since  we heard about this on Friday this is  the type of instability that again I  know that in some way some people are  tired of tired of hearing this other  countries I don't have to name any  country other countries generally  benefit from American instability if  they want to be seen as authorities on  democracy or economic powers or the  countries that we should be dealing with  to figure things out so in all ways this  is a bad idea and it's a separate  question from if we were to go back to  the beginning whether we would want to  adopt a policy closer to free trade or  closer to protectionism we have a status  quo and what we have to consider now is  sort of how these pronouncements how  these decisions will impact that status  quo and this is not good  send me your thoughts I'm on Twitter at  Dee Pakman the show is on Twitter at  David Pakman show we've got a lot to  talk about today including Melania Trump  liked one of our videos and it was about  her I'll tell you about that later  today's program is sponsored in part by  Blink 'us the entire idea behind blinka  stand the service they provide is  perfect for me and I assume really great  for a lot of people in our audience too  if you watch this show you know I read a  lot of nonfiction books and blink astiz  an app that takes nonfiction books and  it distills the most important points  and insights from the book down to a  powerful package of information which  can be read or listened to in about 15  minutes on your mobile device or tablet  you can zip through books really quickly  I still think it's great to absorb the  wisdom of books by reading them slowly  and in their entirety deep reading is  what it's called but not everybody I  know I don't have the time to read all  the non-fiction books that I really want  to so for the ones I can't read blink  astiz the next best thing  huge selection for one  low flat rate you get access to  thousands of books you can switch  between text and audio both online and  offline and you also get personalized  recommendations based on books you've  already chosen join over 4 million  people already using bling cos that made  the iTunes best apps of 2017 list it's  free to try but you can also get a 20%  discount on a yearly membership by  visiting blinkers comm slash Pakman if  you're watching on YouTube we've put a  link in the description underneath the  video again that's blink is comm /p a  que ma n
 

